Acceptance ratio and efficiency against dtvmc normalized to dtvmc50, the step that gives 50%
acceptance. CASINO, He, gwfn cc-pVQZ, Slater only (no Jastrow), runtype vmc,
vmc_decorr_period 1, 1e7 moves per point, runqmc -p 4 one point at a time.

He/EBES   vmc_method 1, electron by electron:           dtvmc50 = 2.10850e-01
He/CBCS   vmc_method 3, configuration by configuration: dtvmc50 = 9.38343e-02

`a<A>` is one point of the grid Casino.vmc_step_graph lays out equally in acceptance,
dtvmc = dtvmc50 * [erfinv(1 - A)/erfinv(1/2)]^2 over the nineteen targets 0.95 ... 0.05, with
opt_dtvmc 0 so the step is the one asked for. `opt` is the run with opt_dtvmc 1: CASINO
optimizes the step only under vmc_method 1, where it returned 2.05520e-01 against the
2.10850e-01 the grid interpolates; under vmc_method 3 it returned the input value untouched, so
dtvmc50 is the interpolated one in both files.

    examples/acceptance_curve/acceptance_curve.py [SYSTEM ...]

reads the out files and writes He-EBES.dat, He-CBCS.dat and He.png, the latter the efficiency
against the acceptance ratio. The acceptance against the step is in the tables.

dtvmc is the variance of the Gaussian proposal, and the sum rule shares it between the
electrons, so EBES should sit at N times the CBCS step: 2.25 measured against N = 2.

What the grid measures is how far the Gaussian law misses the target it was aimed at. It misses
in one direction on each side of 50% and by more the further out the target is:

    target   0.95    0.75    0.55    0.50    0.45    0.25    0.05
    CBCS   -0.004  -0.015  -0.007  -0.002  +0.004  +0.037  +0.063
    EBES   -0.007  -0.023  -0.008  -0.000  +0.009  +0.058  +0.096

The sign flip at 50% is the fat tail of X = ln(Psi'^2/Psi^2): below the target the law
prescribes too small a step and above it too large a one, and only at 50% does the map's fixed
point make the error vanish by construction. At the last target the measurement is 0.113 (CBCS)
and 0.146 (EBES) against the 0.048 and 0.050 the Gaussian law puts there.

The same targets in PyCasino agree with CASINO's numbers to better than 0.001 in acceptance:
examples/time_step/CBCS/He-gto.dat, whose optimized DTVMC is 9.495e-02 against 9.383e-02 here,
gives 0.94571 / 0.49723 / 0.11229 at targets 0.95 / 0.50 / 0.05 against 0.94561 / 0.49810 /
0.11275 here.

The two-parameter form p = C / (C + exp(a*sqrt(dtvmc/dtvmc50)) - 1) fits the whole grid with
C = 0.997, a = 0.684, rms 0.0045 (EBES) and C = 1.403, a = 0.872, rms 0.0028 (CBCS).

The efficiency CASINO prints, 1 / (err^2 * CPU time), peaks at 3.48e5 at an acceptance of 50.0%
for EBES and 1.87e5 on a plateau spanning 45 ... 60% for CBCS, EBES being the faster method by
1.9 at its own optimum. Below 30% acceptance the series are still ragged, the correlation time
being long there and its estimate noisy, but around the maximum the scatter is a few per cent
and both maxima are flat: what this measures is that the optimum sits near 50% for He and that
nothing is lost anywhere in 45 ... 60%. It is not the optimum of examples/step_profile: the
decorrelation period is held at 1 here, while there it is minimized out at every point.

Run one point at a time with runqmc -p 4, never four single-process points at once: independent
mpirun instances each bind their rank 0 to the same physical core -- on this box the four jobs
landed on cpu0 and cpu4, the two threads of core 0 -- and the run then reports half the CPU it
uses, real time twice CPU time, and an efficiency wrong by a factor that depends on how the
jobs happened to pair up.
